{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.VanishingSetUnderdetermines","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.VanishingSetUnderdetermines","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeightVanishing"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaled_two_one_not_weight_system","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaled_two_one_not_weight_system","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L170-L205","doc":"**A concrete function with the right vanishing set that is not an Ocneanu\nweight system.**\n\nTake the level-2 weights at `Q = 2` over `ℚ` — the satisfiability witness\n`isOcneanuWeightSystem_schurPrincipal` — and double the value on the two-row\ndiagram `(2,1)`, the block PR #5185 measured at `y₍₂,₁₎ = q²/(q²+1)² ≠ 0`. The\nresult annihilates exactly the same diagrams (in particular it still kills\n`(1³)` and still spares `(2,1)`), yet it is **not** a weight system at any\nMarkov parameter.\n\nSo a vanishing-set hypothesis admits this function and the genuine one alike,\nwhile `IsOcneanuWeightSystem` separates them. This is the concrete form of the\ngap, on the same two blocks `discriminates_at_n_three` uses. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.vanishingSet_not_determines_weights","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.vanishingSet_not_determines_weights","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L121-L161","doc":"**The vanishing set does not determine the weights.**\n\nGiven *any* genuine Ocneanu weight system `y`, and any diagram `μ₀ ≠ ⊥` that `y`\ndoes not annihilate, the one-point rescaling `rescaleAt μ₀ c y` (with `c ≠ 0`,\n`c ≠ 1`):\n\n* has **exactly** the same vanishing set as `y` — so it satisfies every\n  vanishing-set hypothesis that `y` does; and\n* is **not** an Ocneanu weight system at `(Q, z', r)` for any `z'`.\n\nHence replacing `weight_eq_schurPrincipal` by a vanishing-set field yields a\n*strictly weaker* hypothesis. A downstream consumer of weight **values** — as\nopposed to the vanishing set alone — is not served by the weaker class.\n\nThe proof is rigidity: `rescaleAt μ₀ c y` still agrees with `y` at `⊥` (since\n`μ₀ ≠ ⊥`), so if it were a weight system it would have to equal `y` everywhere\nby `ocneanuWeight_unique_of_bot_eq`; but it differs at `μ₀` because `c ≠ 1` and\n`y μ₀ ≠ 0`. Delete `ocneanuWeight_eq_bot_smul_schurPrincipal` and the argument\ndoes not go through: nothing else forces the value at `μ₀`.\n\n`z` is explicit: it appears in the hypothesis but not in the conclusion, so\ntypeclass resolution cannot recover it (the same convention as\n`ocneanuWeight_eq_zero_iff_of_wenzl`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L110-L119","doc":"**A one-point rescaling preserves the vanishing set exactly.**\n\nFor `c ≠ 0`, `rescaleAt μ₀ c y` vanishes at precisely the diagrams where `y`\ndoes. So a vanishing-set hypothesis cannot tell the two apart. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L106-L108","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt_self","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L102-L104","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.rescaleAt","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L93-L100","doc":"The one-point rescaling of a weight function: multiply the value at `μ₀` by\n`c` and leave every other value alone.\n\n`YoungDiagram` carries no `DecidableEq` instance, so this is classical and\n`noncomputable`; nothing below evaluates it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_unique_of_bot_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_unique_of_bot_eq","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L77-L88","doc":"**Two weight systems at the same `(Q, r)` agreeing at `⊥` agree everywhere.**\n\nAn immediate corollary of rigidity, recorded because it is the form in which the\nuniqueness is wanted below: the class has no room for two genuinely different\nsolutions once the normalisation is fixed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_eq_bot_smul_schurPrincipal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_eq_bot_smul_schurPrincipal","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L60-L75","doc":"**Rigidity — the class determines `y` up to the single scalar `y ⊥`.**\n\n`y μ = y ⊥ · s_μ`. The normaliser `Z` of the class is not a free parameter once\n`y ⊥` is known: it is `1 / y ⊥`.\n\nThis is what `IsOcneanuWeightSystem` supplies *beyond* a vanishing set, and it\nis the whole of the difference — a weight system carries one scalar's worth of\nfreedom, whereas the vanishing set leaves a nonzero scalar free at *every*\nsurviving diagram independently (`vanishingSet_not_determines_weights`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_bot_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.ocneanuWeight_bot_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L50-L58","doc":"**The value at the empty diagram is never zero.**\n\nProved from the class fields rather than from the vanishing criterion, so that\nit does not inherit the genericity hypothesis: `y ⊥ = 1 / Z` with `Z ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal_bot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal_bot","kind":"theorem","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/5697c3130d79ead793a58c1a0a984a2e49b1365f/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/VanishingSetUnderdetermines.lean#L41-L46","doc":"`schurPrincipal` at the empty diagram is `1`: the cell product is empty and\n`n(⊥) = 0`. This is the normalisation point that gives the rigidity lemma below\na canonical scalar to be stated against. ","signatureChunk":0}]}